==English==
{{wp|,<dab!>}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=plant|:inh|enm:fruyt<id:produce>|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰrewHg-}}
{{dercat|en|itc-pro}}
From {{inh|en|enm|fruyt}}, {{m|enm|frut|t=fruits and vegetables}}, from {{der|en|fro|fruit|t=produce, fruits and vegetables}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n}}</ref><ref name="Etymonline n.">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|pos=n}}</ref><ref name="Collins">{{R:Collins}}</ref> from {{der|en|la|fruitus|t=enjoyment, proceeds, profits, produce, income}}, {{m|la|frūctus}}<ref name="Etymonline n." /><ref name="Collins" /> and {{m|la|frūx|t=crop, produce, fruit|ng=compare {{cog|la|fruor|t=have the benefit of, to use, to enjoy}}}}.

{{doublet|en|fructus}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|brook|t=to bear, tolerate}} and {{cog|de|brauchen|t=to need}}. Partially displaced native {{ncog|ang|wæstm}}, {{m|ang|ofett}} and {{m|ang|æppel}} (whence modern [[ovest]] and [[apple]]).

Compare {{cog|nl|vrucht}}, {{cog|de|Frucht}}, {{cog|nb,nn,sv|frukt}}, {{cog|da|frugt}}.<ref name="Etymonline n." />

In the derogatory senses of “crazy person” and “homosexual or effeminate man”, possibly a shortening of {{m|en|fruitcake}}, or of independent origin, compare {{w|Fruit (slang)}}.

