From {{inh|en|enm|fressh}}, from {{inh|en|ang|fersċ|t=fresh, pure, sweet}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*frisk|t=fresh}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*friskaz|t=fresh}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*preysk-|t=fresh}}. The verb is from {{inh|en|enm|freshen|t=to freshen}}, from the adjective.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|fresch|t=fresh}}, {{cog|fy|farsk|t=fresh}}, {{cog|nl|vers|t=fresh}}, {{cog|wa|frexh|t=fresh}}, {{cog|de|frisch|t=fresh}}, {{cog|fr|frais|t=fresh}}, {{cog|da,nb,nn|frisk|t=fresh}}, {{cog|sv|frisk|t=well, fresh}}, {{cog|is|ferskur|t=fresh}}, {{cog|lt|prėskas|t=unflavoured, tasteless, fresh}}, {{cog|ru|пре́сный|t=sweet, fresh, unleavened, tasteless}}. {{doublet|en|fresco|frisk}}.

Slang sense possibly shortened form of “fresh out the pack”, 1980s routine by {{w|Grand Wizzard Theodore}}.<ref>{{cite-book
