#: {{ux|en|There is a '''gap''' between the roof and the gutter.}}
# A vacant space or time.
#: {{ux|en|I have a '''gap''' in my schedule next Tuesday.}}
# A [[hiatus]], a pause in something which is otherwise continuous.
#: {{ux|en|I'm taking a '''gap'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|You must wait for a '''gap''' in the traffic before crossing the road.}}
# A [[vacancy]], [[deficit]], [[absence]], or [[lack]].
#: {{syn|en|blank}}
#: {{ux|en|Their departure has left a '''gap''' in the workforce.}}
#: {{ux|en|Find words to fill the '''gaps''' in an incomplete sentence.}}
#: {{ux|en|She has a '''gap''' in her teeth.}} (see also [[gap-toothed]])
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21582495-computers-will-help-people-understand-brains-better-and-understanding-brains The machine of a new soul]
|passage=The yawning '''gap''' in neuroscientists’ understanding of their topic is in the intermediate scale of the brain’s anatomy. Science has a passable knowledge of how individual nerve cells, known as neurons, work. It also knows which visible lobes and ganglia of the brain do what. But how the neurons are organised in these lobes and ganglia remains obscure. Yet this is the level of organisation that does the actual thinking—and is, presumably, the seat of consciousness.}}
